title: Suspected AI-Assisted AWS Compromise
page: breaches/ai-assisted-aws-72-hours.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Per Sygnia, one financially motivated actor went from an app weakness to broad control of a large AWS estate in roughly 72 hours; AI assistance is inferred, not proven.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: ~72 | hours from initial access to broad cloud compromise | roughly, per Sygnia's case study
stat: 4 | AWS accounts whose keys were used in one observed second | same source IP and user agent, per Sygnia
stat: Dozens | of databases queried | with several hundred unique SQL queries, per Sygnia
The actor is unnamed and financially motivated. AI assistance is inferred, not proven.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with. All three come from Sygnia's case study.

Sygnia's incident response team describes a single financially motivated actor who went from a weakness in an internet-facing application to broad control of a large AWS estate in roughly 72 hours. Keep the word roughly when you say it.

Sygnia reports that in one observed second, four different access keys belonging to four separate AWS accounts were used from the same source IP address and the same user agent.

The actor also ran several hundred unique SQL queries across dozens of databases.

The actor is unnamed. The page calls the AI angle an inference, not a proven fact, and this deck keeps that hedge throughout. AWS is Amazon Web Services.

== What happened, in roughly 72 hours
layout: text
Per Sygnia, one financially motivated actor went from an app weakness to broad control of a large AWS estate.
- No custom malware, no zero-day; extortion without encryption.
- The victim, incident dates and entry vulnerability were withheld.
notes:
Here is the page's summary in brief.

Sygnia's incident response team published a case study describing a single financially motivated actor who went from a weakness in an internet-facing application to broad control of a large AWS estate in roughly 72 hours.

There was no custom malware and no zero-day (a previously unknown vulnerability). Every action mapped to techniques defenders have tracked for years.

Rather than encrypting anything, the actor demonstrated control of the cloud infrastructure itself, by denying S3 bucket access, scaling ECS services to zero, adding network ACL deny rules and purging SQS queues, and used that as extortion leverage.

The victim organization, the incident dates and the initial-access vulnerability were all withheld. That is why this deck has no timeline: the page gives no dates for the stages.

== Why Sygnia suspects AI, and how far it goes
layout: compare
col: Sygnia's basis | Attacker-created scripts and structured reporting artifacts, "consistent with AI-assisted generation" | A self-documenting payload embedded in a malicious commit | Parallelism hard to reconcile with a single human operator
col: Where Sygnia stops | Four keys in one observed second: automated, centrally orchestrated, potentially agent-driven, no more | Several hundred SQL queries: not definitive proof of autonomous AI-agent activity | No specific model, agent framework or vendor identified
The page calls the AI angle an inference, not a proven fact.
notes:
This is the page's central caveat: the AI angle is an inference, not a proven fact.

Sygnia assesses that AI-assisted or agentic workflows played a role, based on three things: attacker-created scripts and structured reporting artifacts it describes as consistent with AI-assisted generation, a self-documenting payload embedded in a malicious commit, and parallelism that is hard to reconcile with a single human operator.

Sygnia hedges again at the stages. Of the four keys used in one observed second, its caveat is that the pattern points to automated, centrally orchestrated, potentially agent-driven execution, and no more than that. Of the SQL queries, it says they are not definitive proof of autonomous AI-agent activity, but consistent with AI-assisted or orchestrated operations that can process context and customize actions at speed.

No specific model, agent framework or vendor was identified.

When you present this case, say suspected, as the page's title does.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | App weakness | T1078
step: 02 | Discovery | Trust mapping | T1580
step: 03 | Credential Access | Secrets sprawl | T1552
step: 04 | Persistence | Footholds | T1059
step: 05 | Execution | Pipeline abuse | T1677
step: 06 | Lateral Movement | Four accounts | T1078.004
step: 07 | Collection & Exfiltration | SQL queries | T1213
step: 08 | Impact | Extortion | T1578
Arrows show the page's stage order. Per the page, each recovered secret fed stage 02 again.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's eight stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is initial access through an application weakness that yielded an AWS access key. Stage 02 is discovery, repeated from every new key. Stage 03 is credential access, where secrets sprawl paid out. Stage 04 is persistence through redundant footholds. Stage 05 is execution through CI/CD pipeline abuse. Stage 06 is lateral movement: four accounts' keys used in one observed second. Stage 07 is collection and exfiltration through several hundred SQL queries. Stage 08 is the impact: extortion by demonstrating control.

Read the arrows as the page's order of stages. The page does describe a loop: every secret recovered at stage 03 fed stage 02 again, with a wider permission scope each time. It gives no times for individual stages, only roughly 72 hours overall.

The next slides take each stage in turn.

== An app weakness handed the actor an AWS key
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1078 | Valid Accounts
fact: Entry point | a weakness in an internet-facing application
fact: Not named by Sygnia | the application, vendor, CVE or victim
fact: Yielded | a long-lived AWS access key for one account
fact: Detection gap | no anomaly on first use from new infrastructure
A valid key makes the attacker a legitimate AWS principal from the first call.
notes:
Stage 01 is initial access, which the page maps to T1078, Valid Accounts.

Sygnia does not name the application, the vendor or a CVE, and does not name the victim. A CVE is an entry in the public Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures list. What the report does say is that the actor obtained an access key to one of the victim's AWS accounts through weaknesses in an internet-facing application. After that, the intrusion followed a familiar shape: access, discovery of exposed credentials, expansion into cloud infrastructure.

Because the entry credential was a legitimate long-lived AWS access key, every API call from that point on was made by an authorized principal. The page gives the detection gap as no anomaly raised on first use of the key from new infrastructure.

The page calls the undisclosed entry point the least interesting part of this chain. The next slide explains what an AWS access key is, in general.

== What an AWS access key is
layout: background
source: AWS IAM User Guide, manage access keys for IAM users | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_access-keys.html
Access keys are long-term credentials for an IAM user or the AWS account root user. An access key ID and a secret access key, used together, sign programmatic requests to the AWS CLI or API.
notes:
Background, from the AWS IAM User Guide, in general terms.

Access keys are long-term credentials for an IAM user or the AWS account root user. You use them to sign programmatic requests to the AWS command line interface or the AWS API, directly or through an AWS SDK. Each key has two parts, an access key ID and a secret access key, and both are used together to authenticate a request.

AWS calls IAM users with access keys an account security risk, and warns that giving your access keys to unauthorized parties might give them permanent access to your account. As a best practice, it recommends temporary security credentials, such as IAM roles, instead of long-term credentials like access keys.

IAM is AWS Identity and Access Management.

== Discovery re-ran on every newly obtained key
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Discovery
technique: T1580 | Cloud Infrastructure Discovery
technique: T1619 | Cloud Storage Object Discovery
fact: Enumerated | IAM permissions, role trust policies, reachable resources
fact: Per Sygnia | identity and trust relationships analyzed for escalation paths
fact: Secrets sweep | ECS and EC2 env vars, S3, Parameter Store, Secrets Manager
fact: Detection gap | high-volume enumeration across accounts, no correlated alert
Each new key restarted the loop: what can this identity do, and what can it reach?
notes:
Stage 02 is discovery. The page maps it to T1580, Cloud Infrastructure Discovery, and also tags T1619, Cloud Storage Object Discovery, shown on the slide, and T1087, Account Discovery, left off for space.

The page calls discovery a subroutine in this intrusion rather than a phase. Each time the actor obtained a new credential, they validated it and repeated the same loop from the new permission scope: what can this identity do, what can it reach, and which trust relationships lead somewhere better.

Sygnia describes the actor analyzing identity relationships, privilege assignments and trust relationships to identify multiple paths for privilege escalation. The same pass swept for secrets across environment variables on ECS containers and EC2 instances, objects in S3, AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store and AWS Secrets Manager.

The page gives the detection gap: high-volume read-only enumeration spread across accounts raised no correlated alert.

ECS is Amazon's container service, EC2 its virtual machines and S3 its object storage. The next slide explains IAM roles and trust policies in general.

== What an IAM role and its trust policy are
layout: background
source: AWS IAM User Guide, IAM roles | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_terms-and-concepts.html
An IAM role is an identity with permissions but no long-term credentials; assuming it gives temporary ones. Its trust policy, which every role needs, names the principals trusted to assume it.
notes:
Background, from the AWS IAM User Guide, in general terms.

An IAM role is an identity in an AWS account with permission policies that determine what it can and cannot do. Unlike an IAM user, a role is meant to be assumable by anyone who needs it, and it has no standard long-term credentials such as a password or access keys. Assuming a role provides temporary security credentials for a role session.

A role trust policy is a JSON policy document, a required resource-based policy attached to the role, in which you define the principals that you trust to assume it. Those principals can include users, roles, accounts and services.

A principal is an entity in AWS that can perform actions and access resources, such as the account root user, an IAM user or a role.

== Plaintext secrets recovered from S3 and Parameter Store, entries from Secrets Manager
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552 | Unsecured Credentials
technique: T1528 | Steal Application Access Token
fact: Recovered from | S3 objects (plaintext), Parameter Store, Secrets Manager
fact: Also from | env vars, GitHub and Bitbucket runners, app database rows
fact: Why it worked | the same material in storage, config and runtime layers
fact: Effect | every recovered secret fed stage 02 again, with a wider scope
The same material sat in several layers at once, so no single control gated it.
notes:
Stage 03 is credential access. The page maps it to T1552, Unsecured Credentials, and also tags T1528, Steal Application Access Token.

The page says Sygnia's list of where secrets were found is the whole lesson in one sentence: environment variables from ECS containers and EC2 instances, CI/CD runner environments for both GitHub and Bitbucket, plaintext secrets stored in S3 buckets, API keys in application databases, secrets in AWS Secrets Manager, and parameters in SSM Parameter Store. CI/CD stands for continuous integration and continuous delivery.

The same credential material sat in several of those layers at once, so no single control gated it. Rotating an entry in Secrets Manager did nothing about the copy pasted into an S3 object or baked into a container's environment block.

Harvested credentials let the actor keep restarting the discovery loop of stage 02, with a wider scope each time. The page's detection gap: bulk GetParameter and GetSecretValue reads, the calls that read a parameter and a secret, were not baselined per identity.

The next slide explains Parameter Store and Secrets Manager in general.

== What Parameter Store and Secrets Manager hold
layout: background
source: AWS Systems Manager User Guide, Parameter Store | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/systems-manager-parameter-store.html
credit: Also from What is AWS Secrets Manager? (docs.aws.amazon.com)
Parameter Store holds named configuration values, called parameters, as plain text or encrypted. Secrets Manager stores secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and can rotate them on a schedule.
notes:
Background, from AWS documentation, in general terms.

Parameter Store, a tool in AWS Systems Manager, is a centralized store for named configuration values called parameters. A String parameter holds a plain text value, and a SecureString parameter is encrypted with AWS Key Management Service. For secrets such as database credentials, API keys or tokens, AWS recommends Secrets Manager instead.

AWS Secrets Manager helps you manage, retrieve and rotate database credentials, application credentials, OAuth tokens, API keys and other secrets. An application can retrieve a secret with a call at runtime instead of holding a hard-coded credential, and you can configure an automatic rotation schedule.

Also from What is AWS Secrets Manager?: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html

== Persistence here was part of the extortion strategy
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Persistence
technique: T1059 | Command and Scripting Interpreter
technique: T1651 | Cloud Administration Command
fact: Identity | additional IAM users and access keys created
fact: Runtime | reverse shells on EC2 instances and ECS containers
fact: Redeploy | deployment files modified, so a clean rebuild reinstated access
fact: Application | app-layer users created with elevated permissions
Sygnia: the more durable and distributed the access, the more credible the threat.
notes:
Stage 04 is persistence. The page maps it to T1059, Command and Scripting Interpreter, and also tags T1651, Cloud Administration Command, T1609, Container Administration Command, and T1648, Serverless Execution. The slide shows the first two.

The page treats persistence here as part of the extortion strategy. Sygnia is explicit that the more durable and distributed the access became, the more credible the actor's ability to disrupt, damage or influence recovery.

The actor created additional IAM users and access keys, established reverse shells on EC2 instances and ECS containers, modified deployment files so that a redeploy would reintroduce the foothold, and created application users with elevated permissions. A reverse shell is a command session that a compromised machine opens back to the attacker.

None of it needed malware. Execution rode cloud-native primitives: scripting, cloud administration commands, and container and serverless execution. The page says that is why endpoint tooling had so little to say about it.

== Pipeline abuse, and a “pentest” cover story
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Execution
technique: T1677 | Poisoned Pipeline Execution
fact: Harvested | GitHub and Bitbucket runner environments, for secrets
fact: Modified | repositories and deployment files
fact: Carried by | the pipeline itself, into the cloud
fact: Cover story | a "pentest" or "red team" exercise, "approved" by a CEO who did not exist
Pipeline changes run with the pipeline's own privileges and outlive key rotation.
notes:
Stage 05 is execution through the source-control and CI/CD layer. Sygnia labels this CI/CD pipeline abuse and maps it to T1677, which MITRE publishes under the name Poisoned Pipeline Execution.

The actor used that layer in both directions. Runner environments on GitHub and Bitbucket were harvested for secrets, and repositories plus deployment files were modified so the pipeline itself would carry the changes into the cloud. The page calls this the persistence class that survives credential rotation, because reintroduction happens through an approved deployment.

This is also where the cover story appears. Multiple attacker-created artifacts were framed as a pentest or a red team exercise, in branch names and commit messages, including references suggesting approval by a CEO who did not exist. A pentest is a penetration test, an authorized simulated attack.

The next slide explains poisoned pipeline execution in general, and the one after it sets out the two readings Sygnia gives for the cover story.

== What ATT&CK calls Poisoned Pipeline Execution
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1677 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1677/
Adversaries may manipulate CI/CD processes by injecting malicious code into the build process. ATT&CK describes editing the CI configuration file directly, or editing files that configuration references, such as scripts.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Poisoned Pipeline Execution as adversaries manipulating CI/CD processes by injecting malicious code into the build process, and it lists several mechanisms.

In a direct pipeline execution scenario, the threat actor modifies the CI configuration file itself, such as gitlab-ci.yml in GitLab. In an indirect scenario, the actor injects malicious code into files that the CI configuration file references, such as makefiles, scripts, unit tests and linters.

The page cites this ATT&CK entry among its primary sources. It does not say which mechanism the actor used.

== Why a “pentest” cover story? Sygnia offers two readings
layout: compare
col: What was framed | Branch names and commit messages | A "pentest" or "red team" exercise | Suggested approval by a CEO who did not exist
col: Reading one | It may have been meant to mislead investigators
col: Reading two | It may have been meant to influence AI-assisted tooling | Presenting the work as an authorized penetration test, to reduce refusals
Sygnia commits to neither. The same "red team" framing appeared in an HTML file dropped into S3 buckets at stage 08.
notes:
Sygnia offers two readings of the cover story and commits to neither.

The framing may have been meant to mislead investigators. Or it may have been meant to influence AI-assisted tooling, by presenting the work as an authorized penetration test in order to reduce refusals. A refusal is an AI tool declining a request.

Keep both readings when you present this. The page does not choose between them.

The same red team framing appears once more, at stage 08: an HTML file dropped into S3 buckets carried the framing seen in the repositories.

== Four keys, four accounts, one observed second
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Per Sygnia | 4 access keys, 4 AWS accounts, 1 second
fact: Shared | one source IP address and one user agent
fact: Sygnia's reading | very hard to explain as manual activity
fact: Why it worked | each account's trail showed only its own slice
Sygnia's caveat: the pattern points to automated, centrally orchestrated, potentially agent-driven execution, and no more than that.
notes:
This is the observation the page says the whole case study turns on. The page maps it to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts.

Sygnia reports that in one observed second, four different access keys belonging to four separate accounts were used from the same source IP address and the same user agent. It calls that pattern very hard to explain as manual activity.

Alongside the raw concurrency, the actor kept operational context across many credentials: which key belonged to which workstream, what each one could reach, where it had already been used, and when to abandon it. The page says that state management turned a linear attack path into overlapping waves.

Each account's trail showed only its own slice, so the shared client fingerprint stayed invisible. The page's detection primitive is to correlate CloudTrail, AWS's log of API calls, across accounts on source IP address plus user agent, never per account.

Repeat Sygnia's caveat with the figure. The next slide explains those CloudTrail fields in general.

== What CloudTrail records about each call
layout: background
source: AWS CloudTrail User Guide, CloudTrail record contents | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-event-reference-record-contents.html
credit: Also from Creating a trail for an organization (docs.aws.amazon.com)
A CloudTrail event records who made a request and when, the IP address it came from (sourceIPAddress) and the agent it came through (userAgent), such as the AWS CLI or an SDK.
notes:
Background, from the AWS CloudTrail User Guide, in general terms.

A CloudTrail record holds fields that describe the requested action, and when and where the request was made. userIdentity describes the IAM identity that made the request, and eventTime says when the request was completed.

sourceIPAddress is the IP address the request was made from. For a request from an AWS service, only the service's DNS name is shown. userAgent is the agent through which the request was made, such as the AWS Management Console, an AWS service, the AWS SDKs or the AWS CLI.

If you have an organization in AWS Organizations, you can create an organization trail, which logs events for the management account and all member accounts in the organization. The management account, or a delegated administrator, can create one.

Also from Creating a trail for an organization: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/creating-trail-organization.html

== Several hundred unique SQL queries across dozens of RDS databases
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Collection & Exfiltration
technique: T1213 | Data from Information Repositories
fact: Targeted | user records, transaction data, other sensitive business data
fact: Signal | schema enumeration, then targeted extraction, database after database
fact: Detection gap | no baseline on query volume or novel query shapes
Sygnia: not definitive proof of autonomous AI-agent activity, but consistent with AI-assisted or orchestrated operations.
notes:
Stage 07 is collection and exfiltration. The page maps it to T1213, Data from Information Repositories.

RDS databases were queried for user data, transaction information and other potentially sensitive records, which the page says shows the actor wanted data as well as infrastructure control. RDS is Amazon Relational Database Service.

Sygnia treats the volume and specificity as evidence of rapid environment-specific adaptation: several hundred unique SQL queries across dozens of databases, rapidly enumerating schemas and identifying relevant data. The page adds that writing that many targeted queries against unfamiliar schemas is normally slow, careful work.

Keep Sygnia's hedge. It calls this not definitive proof of autonomous AI-agent activity, but consistent with AI-assisted or orchestrated operations that can process context and customize actions at speed.

The page gives the detection gap as no baseline on query volume or novel query shapes against production RDS.

== Extortion by demonstration, not encryption
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Impact
technique: T1578 | Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure
fact: Actions | S3 access denied, ECS at zero, network ACL denies, SQS purges
fact: Per Sygnia | mostly reversible, performed as a demonstration of capability
fact: Also dropped | an HTML file in S3 buckets, framed as "red team" work
fact: Leverage | control of the infrastructure itself; no encryption involved
Each impact action was a routine cloud admin call, made with valid credentials.
notes:
Stage 08 is the impact. The page maps it to T1578, Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure.

The page notes there is no clean cloud equivalent of an on-premises encryptor, so the actor built leverage a different way. Sygnia describes mostly reversible impact actions performed as a demonstration of capability: denying access to S3 buckets, limiting ECS services or containers to a maximum capacity of zero, creating ACL rules to block network access, and purging SQS queues. ACL stands for access control list, and SQS is Amazon Simple Queue Service.

An HTML file was also dropped into S3 buckets, carrying the same red team framing seen in the repositories at stage 05.

The page says the unsettling part is that every one of these is a routine cloud administration call. The impact ran through the control plane, with valid credentials, using APIs the platform team uses daily. The control plane is the set of APIs that create and configure cloud resources. Without behavioral baselining, the page says, destructive control-plane calls look identical to routine operations.

== Familiar techniques, run fast and in parallel
layout: compare
col: Familiar, per the page | No custom malware and no zero-day | Every action mapped to techniques tracked for years | Impact through routine cloud administration calls
col: Fast and parallel, per Sygnia | Roughly 72 hours to broad cloud compromise | Four accounts' keys in one observed second | Several hundred unique SQL queries, rapidly enumerating schemas
The page says programs sized for multi-week dwell time are obsolete against parallel workstreams across accounts.
notes:
This slide sets two things the page says side by side: the techniques were familiar, and they ran fast and in parallel.

There was no custom malware and no zero-day, and every action mapped to techniques defenders have tracked for years. Even the impact ran through routine cloud administration calls.

What stands out is speed and parallelism, as Sygnia reports it: roughly 72 hours from initial access to broad cloud compromise, keys from four separate accounts used in one observed second, and several hundred unique SQL queries rapidly enumerating schemas across dozens of databases.

The page draws the defender's conclusion: programs sized for multi-week dwell time are obsolete against an actor running parallel workstreams across accounts. Dwell time is how long an attacker is in your environment before detection. Sygnia frames the same shift as moving from a linear response model to a momentum-based one, where investigation and containment run in parallel.

== Six defenses against eight stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07 | 08
row: Correlate CloudTrail across accounts | 06
row: Fix secrets sprawl before rotating | 03
row: Retire long-lived access keys | 01 | 03 | 05
row: GuardDuty findings for this behavior | 02 | 03 | 04
row: SCPs to cap the blast radius | 04 | 06
row: Detection budget in hours, not weeks | 05 | 06
No defense on the page is tied to stage 07, the RDS queries, or to stage 08, the impact.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the eight stages. A cell is marked only where the defense's own words name something only that stage has.

Correlating CloudTrail across accounts answers stage 06: four keys, four accounts, one source IP, one user agent, one second. Fixing secrets sprawl answers stage 03, where the same material sat in several layers at once. Retiring long-lived keys answers stage 01's static access key, and its OIDC federation keeps keys out of CI, whose runner environments stages 03 and 05 say were harvested.

The GuardDuty findings answer what the page calls the enumerate, harvest, persist loop, stages 02, 03 and 04. The SCP defense denies iam:CreateUser and iam:CreateAccessKey, behind stage 04's new IAM users and keys, and its heading says one key cannot become four accounts, which is stage 06. Rebuilding detection around hours names stage 06's parallel workstreams, and its pre-authorized containment names a pipeline freeze, for stage 05.

Now look at the empty columns. No defense on the page is tied to stage 07, the RDS queries, or to stage 08's impact actions. The page does not tie its IP allowlisting on the cloud control plane to stage 08. The stage 07 gap is a discussion question at the end.

== Correlate CloudTrail across accounts, not one at a time
layout: compare
col: One account's trail | Shows only its own slice | The shared client fingerprint stays invisible
col: One organization trail | Every account's trail, in a dedicated log account | Detection written at the organization level | Joined on source IP address plus user agent
The page says one client fingerprint driving keys from different accounts is not a human.
notes:
The page leads its defenses with this one, and calls the cross-account join the single finding that defines this case: four keys, four accounts, one source IP, one user agent, one second.

Each account's trail showed only its own slice, so the shared client fingerprint stayed invisible. Ship every account's trail into a single organization trail in a dedicated log account, and write the detection at the organization level, correlating on source IP address plus user agent.

The page puts it plainly: one client fingerprint driving credentials that belong to different accounts is not a human, and no per-account view will ever surface it. Hold that beside Sygnia's own caveat at stage 06, that the pattern points to automated, centrally orchestrated, potentially agent-driven execution, and no more than that.

== Fix secrets sprawl, and retire long-lived keys
layout: compare
col: Fix sprawl first (stage 03) | Consolidate on Secrets Manager, with automatic rotation | Replace environment-variable injection with runtime retrieval | Scan IaC repositories and S3 for secrets, as a recurring job
col: Retire long-lived keys (01, 03, 05) | IAM Roles Anywhere for workloads outside AWS | OIDC federation for GitHub Actions and Bitbucket Pipelines | IAM roles inside AWS; IMDSv2 required, hop limit 1 where containers run
The page says the whole chain hangs off a static AWS access key.
notes:
Two defenses aim at the credentials themselves.

Fix secrets sprawl before you bother rotating anything. The same credential material sat in ECS and EC2 environment variables, S3 objects, SSM Parameter Store and Secrets Manager at the same time, so no single control gated it. Consolidate on AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation enabled, replace environment-variable injection with runtime retrieval, and run secret scanning over IaC repositories and S3 as a recurring job rather than a one-off cleanup. IaC is infrastructure as code.

Retire long-lived access keys entirely, because the whole chain hangs off a static AWS access key. Use IAM Roles Anywhere for workloads outside AWS, OIDC federation for GitHub Actions and Bitbucket Pipelines so no key is ever stored in CI, and IAM roles for anything running inside AWS. OIDC federation lets a workload trade a short-lived token from its host for temporary cloud credentials.

The page also says to enforce IMDSv2 with HttpTokens set to required, and to set the metadata hop limit to 1 on instances that host containers. IMDS is the instance metadata service. No stage on the page mentions it.

== Turn on the GuardDuty findings that fit this behavior
layout: table
row: GuardDuty finding types | What they catch, per the page
row: InstanceCredentialExfiltration, InsideAWS and OutsideAWS | Instance role credentials used from somewhere they should not be
row: IAMUser/AnomalousBehavior under Discovery, CredentialAccess and Persistence | The enumerate, harvest, persist loop that re-ran on every key
Turn GuardDuty on in every region of every account through the delegated administrator, and route findings into Security Hub.
notes:
Enable the GuardDuty findings that, the page says, describe exactly this behavior. GuardDuty is AWS's threat detection service.

UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.InsideAWS and .OutsideAWS catch instance role credentials used from somewhere they should not be. No stage on the page mentions instance role credentials, so the matrix ties this part to no stage.

Discovery:IAMUser/AnomalousBehavior, CredentialAccess:IAMUser/AnomalousBehavior and Persistence:IAMUser/AnomalousBehavior catch the enumerate, harvest, persist loop that re-ran on every key here. Their names match the phase labels of stages 02, 03 and 04.

Turn GuardDuty on in every region of every account through the delegated administrator, and route findings into Security Hub, AWS's service for gathering security findings in one place.

== Cap the blast radius with SCPs; plan for hours, not weeks
layout: compare
col: SCPs, where not needed (04, 06) | Deny iam:CreateUser and iam:CreateAccessKey outside a break-glass path | Deny ec2:ModifyInstanceMetadataOptions | Deny deleting or reconfiguring CloudTrail and GuardDuty
col: Hours, not weeks (05, 06) | Pre-authorize key disablement and session revocation | Also IP allowlisting on the control plane, and a pipeline freeze | So on-call can act before scoping is complete; rehearse it
The page calls an SCP the one control a compromised account's own administrator cannot switch off.
notes:
Two more defenses.

Cap the blast radius with SCPs, so one key cannot become four accounts. Service control policies, or SCPs, are AWS Organizations guardrails that set the maximum permissions a member account can have. An OU is an organizational unit, a group of accounts. At the OU level, deny the actions used to build durable footholds wherever they are not needed: iam:CreateUser and iam:CreateAccessKey outside a break-glass path, ec2:ModifyInstanceMetadataOptions, and any deletion or reconfiguration of CloudTrail and GuardDuty. A break-glass path is a tightly held emergency route.

Rebuild the detection budget around hours, not weeks. Programs sized for multi-week dwell time are obsolete against an actor running parallel workstreams across accounts. Pre-authorize containment, meaning key disablement, session revocation, IP allowlisting on the cloud control plane and a pipeline freeze, so the on-call engineer can act before scoping is complete, and rehearse it. Sygnia frames the same shift as moving from a linear response model to a momentum-based one, where investigation and containment run in parallel.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Does one detection join every account's CloudTrail on source IP and user agent?
check: Could you list every place one secret is stored, from S3 to environment variables?
check: Do any long-lived access keys remain, in CI or anywhere else?
check: Is GuardDuty on in every region of every account, feeding Security Hub?
check: Do SCPs deny iam:CreateUser and iam:CreateAccessKey outside a break-glass path?
check: Can on-call disable keys, revoke sessions and freeze pipelines before scoping ends?
check: Have you rehearsed that containment?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether any one detection reads every account's CloudTrail, in an organization trail, and joins on source IP address plus user agent. Ask whether you could list every place one secret is stored, across S3 objects, environment variables, Parameter Store and Secrets Manager.

Ask whether any long-lived access keys remain, in CI or anywhere else, and whether instances require IMDSv2. Ask whether GuardDuty is on in every region of every account, with findings routed into Security Hub.

Ask whether SCPs deny iam:CreateUser and iam:CreateAccessKey outside a break-glass path. Finally, ask whether the on-call engineer is pre-authorized to disable keys, revoke sessions, allowlist IPs on the control plane and freeze pipelines before scoping is complete, and whether that has been rehearsed.

== Would anything you run see four accounts' keys in one second?
layout: question
Sygnia reports four access keys, from four separate AWS accounts, used in one observed second from one source IP and one user agent.

Where in your logging would that show up, and who would see it?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

This is the observation the page says the whole case study turns on. Each account's trail showed only its own slice, so the shared client fingerprint stayed invisible.

Ask each person to name where, in their own environment, the four keys' calls would land, and whether any single person or detection sees all four together. Then show the answer slide, which gives what the page says.

== What the page says about the cross-account join
layout: answer
The page says no per-account view will ever surface it.
- Ship every account's trail into one organization trail, in a dedicated log account.
- Write the detection at the organization level, on source IP plus user agent.
notes:
The page's answer is a cross-account join.

No per-account view will ever surface the pattern, the page says, because each account's trail shows only its own slice. Ship every account's trail into a single organization trail in a dedicated log account, and write the detection at the organization level, correlating on source IP address plus user agent.

The page puts it plainly: one client fingerprint driving credentials that belong to different accounts is not a human. Keep Sygnia's caveat with it: the pattern points to automated, centrally orchestrated, potentially agent-driven execution, and no more than that.

Bring the group's answers back to this. If their trails sit in separate accounts, the page's guidance is one organization trail, with the detection written there.

== What would you add for stage 07?
layout: question
The page ties no defense to stage 07, where several hundred unique SQL queries ran across dozens of RDS databases.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from an empty column in the defense matrix.

Two columns are empty, 07 and 08, because no defense on the page names stage 07's RDS queries or stage 08's actions on S3, ECS, network ACLs and SQS queues. The page's containment list includes IP allowlisting on the cloud control plane, but the page does not tie it to stage 08. This question takes stage 07.

The page ties no defense to stage 07. There, RDS databases were queried for user data, transaction information and other potentially sensitive records: several hundred unique SQL queries across dozens of databases, rapidly enumerating schemas and identifying relevant data.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stage 07
layout: answer
Any control you propose is your own suggestion. Test it against the reported details:
- Several hundred unique SQL queries across dozens of RDS databases.
- Schema enumeration, then targeted extraction, database after database.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 07, and this deck adds none of its own.

Stage 07 does name what was missing, as a detection gap rather than a defense: no baseline on query volume or novel query shapes against production RDS.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each one against the facts the page reports: several hundred unique SQL queries across dozens of RDS databases, schema enumeration followed immediately by targeted extraction, and queries for user records, transaction data and other sensitive business data.

Keep Sygnia's hedge in the room too. It calls the query pattern not definitive proof of autonomous AI-agent activity, so a proposal should say what it would catch whoever, or whatever, wrote the queries.

